   V. BLACK-BOX STRENGTH AND SCORE VALIDATION

   A direct UCI-only comparison was rerun against the official
Stockfish 19 Linux x86-64 universal binary. On the existing
120 legal real-game positions at depth 10, the homemade mode
measured 178.925 cp mean absolute error, 103 cp median
absolute error, and 1,204 cp maximum absolute error. These
values are consistent with the inherited score-parity measure-
ment and show that the optimization preserved behavior rather
than changing the model’s calibration.

   A fixed-control smoke tournament used one game per
variant at 30, 100, and 300 ms per move, with an 80-ply cap.
The nonlinear mode drew all three sampled games against
Stockfish 19. The sample is far too small for an Elo claim,
but it provides a protocol-level regression check: the optimized
mode produced legal play and did not exhibit an immediate
stability failure.

         VI. PARITY ASSESSMENT AND LIMITATIONS

   The project should not claim SFNNv16 parity from these
results. The 300,000-position corpus is a data-scale milestone,
not a trained neural model. The residual remains a handcrafted
nonlinear correction over the HCE backbone, and the 120-
position score corpus remains the primary direct calibration
set. The present evidence supports a practical parity estimate
of approximately 23/100, up from the inherited 20/100 as-
sessment, reflecting improved inference integration and re-
producibility rather than a demonstrated strength leap. The
requested 50/100 milestone remains open.

   The next technically justified step is black-box teacher label-
ing of the leakage-safe train and validation splits, followed by
a compact original model with integer feature transforms, per-
layer scales, and calibration against held-out games. Any parity
claim should then report held-out MAE, rank correlation,

